5BSD Imager 0.2
==============

A native Dart/Flutter USB image writer, using the 5BSD toolkit ports.
The interface uses macos_ui 2.2.2: a light macOS-style window, traffic-light
window buttons, push buttons, checkboxes, progress bars, and confirmation
sheets. Red and gold accents retain the 5BSD identity. Cupertino icons are
bundled; text uses available system fonts rather than redistributing Apple
fonts. Keyboard adapters add activation and visible focus to buttons.
macOS-only native plugins are not invoked on 5BSD; GTK handles the window
and file picker, and polkit handles authentication. The footer reads
"Built for 5BSD ❤️ · #livefreeordie". The window has a custom draggable title bar. Source is
maintained here in the port. The GTK runner registers a hidden custom titlebar
before window realization so GTK 3 requests client-side decorations on KWin;
set_decorated(FALSE) alone does not negotiate this on Wayland. Only Flutter
draws the visible header, and the original GDK events drive window movement
and resizing. Chrome events are intercepted on FlView's child event box,
before Flutter consumes button presses; a handler on FlView itself never
receives those consumed events. Wayland traces confirmed KWin accepts client
mode (1).
The runner handles delete-event before Flutter's GApplication exit hook:
idle windows are destroyed to stop gtk_main, while busy writes refuse close.

Supported workflow
------------------
Choose an .img, .iso, .raw, or XZ-compressed disk image; select one or more
whole drives; confirm erasure; authorize through the desktop polkit agent;
write and verify. Drive discovery reads public GEOM metadata without asking
for a password. Writing requests authorization for each target; prompts are
serialized while authorized writes run concurrently. Authorization is not
retained. No operator/raw-disk group or relaxed /dev permissions are needed.

Capacity is shown in decimal GB and binary GiB. Partitions can be expanded
for context but cannot be selected individually. Protected disks are behind
an expandable row. The list updates automatically every three seconds when
idle. Confirmation identifies the image, all selected drives, their sizes,
serial numbers and partition counts. Write and verification progress are
shown separately, with per-drive status for a batch.

XZ is recognized by signature, including renamed images. The base-system
/usr/bin/xz reads its expanded size and streams decompressed bytes as the
normal user. No temporary expanded image is required. Capacity checks use
the expanded size; decoder failure, checksum failure, truncation or changed
size prevents success. XZ environment overrides are cleared and decoder
memory is limited to 512 MiB per target. Cancellation stops each decoder and
writer. Tar archives (.tar.xz/.txz) are not disk images and are rejected.

Only whole da disks attached through umass are eligible. Mounted disks,
active pools, swap, and other open GEOM consumers are refused. Unmount drives
yourself before scanning again. Internal SATA/NVMe/SCSI disks, SD controllers,
other compression formats, automatic unmounting, and downloading are not
supported. An ISO must already support booting from USB;
the app does not convert arbitrary optical-disc ISOs into hybrid images.
The kernel's GEOM attachment uses the CAM name umass-simN (for example,
umass-sim0), which differs from the USB device name umassN. Both recognized
forms require an exact numeric unit suffix; this is covered by regression tests.

Read-back verification covers the image bytes and zero padding through the
last sector. It does not erase or verify unused space after the image. Writing
an image is not a secure erase operation. Cancellation, disconnects, or errors
can leave an incomplete drive. Every completed write must pass SHA-256 read-back verification. Cache flushing
is attempted first. If GEOM explicitly reports EOPNOTSUPP, verification still
runs and the UI shows a cache-flush notice; persistence after unplugging has
not been confirmed. The first EINVAL is retried once because CAM can learn
that SYNCHRONIZE CACHE is unsupported and then report EOPNOTSUPP. Other sync
or flush errors fail the operation. No kernel flush policy is changed.

Privilege boundary
------------------
The GUI runs as the desktop user. It opens the image and streams bytes to a
fixed installed helper through pkexec; no image pathname or shell command is
passed to root. Passwords are handled by the desktop's authentication agent,
never collected by Dart, stored, or passed in argv or environment variables.
The helper accepts only --list and --write disk identity byte-count.

Before the first write, the helper checks the GEOM provider identity, serial
when available, geometry, USB attachment, open-consumer counts, and kernel
write protections. It checks again against the held device descriptor. It
rejects kern.geom.debugflags foot-shooting mode. This is not an O_EXCL claim:
GEOM does not implement O_EXCL on these device nodes. GEOM write access blocks
normal exclusive consumers such as mounted filesystems. Another privileged
raw writer can still interfere; do not run other disk tools concurrently.

After opening and validating the target, the helper clears supplementary
groups, drops root to the authenticated caller, closes other descriptors,
limits descriptor rights and ioctl access, and enters Capsicum. The only
device ioctl retained is DIOCGFLUSH. It then consumes image bytes. Closing
the input pipe cancels writing or verification; broken output also aborts.

Build and install the port
--------------------------
Use the existing ports checkout (currently /home/koryheard/5BSD-ports), not
the empty /usr/ports directory. Normal ports dependency handling supplies
lang/dart, x11-toolkits/flutter-engine, GTK 3, and polkit. All source archives,
pub packages, and fonts are checksummed; application compilation is offline.
pub-packages.json pins the hosted dependency closure and archive hashes.
The port extracts these packages into an isolated PUB_CACHE; a development
build needs the same pinned packages and hash files in its PUB_CACHE.
The build uses the host CPU count for independent native compilation jobs.

From a root shell, with the ports dependencies available:

  cd /home/koryheard/5BSD-ports/sysutils/5bsd-imager
  make PORTSDIR=/home/koryheard/5BSD-ports install

Run `5bsd-imager` as your regular desktop user. A running system D-Bus,
polkit service, and desktop authentication agent are required. Plasma's
polkit agent works with this integration. The GUI refuses to run as root.
The package installs the helper and policy as root-owned system files; it
does not install a setuid binary or change sudo/doas rules.

Local development build used on this workstation
------------------------------------------------
  /usr/local/bin/python3.12 files/app/build.py \
    --flutter /home/koryheard/5bsd-flutter \
    --dart /home/koryheard/5bsd-flutter/engine/src/out/host_release_native/dart-sdk/bin/dart \
    --engine-out /home/koryheard/5bsd-flutter/engine/src/out/host_release_native \
    --fonts /home/koryheard/5bsd-flutter/out/5bsd-app-packages/material-fonts.zip \
    --output work/runtime

Launch work/runtime/5bsd-imager normally. This development bundle contains
its matching engine. --smoke-test renders the window, exports the app's own
view to a newly created temporary directory, and exits after ten seconds.
It reads the public disk inventory but cannot select images or drives, write,
or request authentication. Add --preview-progress for a clearly labeled
synthetic two-drive progress preview, or --preview-confirmation for the erase
confirmation design. These modes do not run the helper or allow disk writes.

For functional testing of this development bundle, installing only the
helper and policy requires a root shell because /usr/local/libexec and the
system policy directory must be administrator-owned:

  cd /home/koryheard/5BSD-ports/sysutils/5bsd-imager
  install -d -o root -g wheel -m 0755 /usr/local/libexec
  install -d -o root -g wheel -m 0755 /usr/local/share/polkit-1/actions
  install -o root -g wheel -m 0555 work/runtime/5bsd-imager-helper /usr/local/libexec/5bsd-imager-helper
  install -o root -g wheel -m 0644 work/runtime/org.fivebsd.Imager.policy /usr/local/share/polkit-1/actions/org.fivebsd.Imager.policy

Do not point the polkit action at an executable in a user-writable build
directory. Install the helper at its fixed system path before trying the
drive workflow. These commands do not flash anything.

Validation
----------
  /usr/local/bin/python3.12 files/app/tests/run.py --work work/tests
  dart files/app/tests/writer_test.dart

The test-only driver refuses device nodes. It tests disk-name and transport
validation, busy-state checks, size/overflow/sector rules, JSON encoding,
sector padding, multi-chunk transfers, read-back corruption, truncated input,
and cancellation, using temporary regular files. Its executable is not
installed and production has no arbitrary-file write or test mode.

Dart tests use fake unprivileged writer processes to check concurrent jobs,
authorization failure isolation, cancellation, strict completion/verification,
and changed-source / duplicate-target / protected-drive guards. XZ fixtures
check exact decoded content on multiple writers, expanded capacity, corruption,
truncation, and invalid signatures. No test writes device nodes.

Native AOT compilation, GUI rendering, policy/desktop-file validation, and
staging manifest checks were also run. Real SanDisk USB discovery and polkit
prompting were tested. On 2026-10-07, an authorized physical test wrote a generated 512 MiB + 37 byte
XZ image to the 123 GB SanDisk Ultra and verified all 536,871,424 padded bytes
with SHA-256 through the production Dart writer and installed polkit helper.
The helper was observed running as UID 1001 in Capsicum. The test took 22
seconds including authorization. This drive does not support SYNCHRONIZE
CACHE; the test exposed the initial abort and validated the explicit
unsupported-flush notice after the fix. Other flush errors remain fatal.
After a physical unplug/reconnect, a separate read-only hash check also
matched every padded byte (SHA-256
ccb7569ae9d4ee4d8317ecefa56756ff0132776f171edb7c92ec968282942594).
This tests the hardware pipeline; it is not a boot test or GUI click automation.
